# NARROWED 2026-09-16 (panel 156 R1 and R5, defect 047). It asserted
# `called from node_value` and that was the runtime's own FAILURE:
# `hero_stack_blame` returns the `dladdr` symbol at the faulting pc when
# the frame walk finds no Heroes name, so the row pinned the fallback.
# The name is also an OPTIMISATION-level fact, not a platform one — the
# same program on the same Mac says `node_value` at -O0 and `main` at
# -O2, because `h_main_main` is inlined away — so no symbol may be
# pinned. The contract is the sentence and the offset; `called from` is
# best effort and Windows has no frame walk to produce one.
#
# `out_is("7\n")` STAYS and is now stronger, not weaker: it was true
# only here until `parts/os.c` made stdout survive an abort on every
# platform, and it is measured true on all three.

# Panel 099 R5 (M-robustness-guards step 6): a nested program, its leaf
# compiled directly. The root program runs; the leaf alone refuses and
# says where it looked and which file decided that; and beside a decoy
# `lib/util.hero` the leaf alone checks CLEAN against a different module
# — the hazard the root base carries, stated by a row rather than by a
# silence (the historian's standing prediction is Zig #13970's shape).

# A hole is not an error and not a binary: the report is the
# artifact, and `build` is the one verb that must refuse.

# `heroes test` runs each block and reports one line each.

# Modules: every one names itself in the emitted C, a file with no
# main is the author's error, and a diagnostic in another module
# says which file it is in — in both spellings.

# Acceptance row 4, as panel 093 R5 re-read it. The caller is
# correct and the mistake is one module over, so what these pin is
# that the verdict finds the AUTHOR's line in the declaring module
# rather than blaming whichever translation unit was compiling.

# Panel 094's refutation, kept as a program. One C symbol bound at
# two signatures in two modules: the probes are mangled per module
# and the call goes through the header's own prototype, so C sees
# one `printf` and Heroes sees two bindings. The claim this refutes
# — that a C name carries one arity — was the whole stated case for
# adding a rename clause to the language.

# `mutate` refuses a corpus that does not compile, and prints the
# survivors of one operator when asked for them.
# The corpus that cannot compile is `tests/golden/check/` — 65 programs
# the compiler is SUPPOSED to refuse. It was the bootstrap's library
# directory until M-bootstrap-archive, which is a directory that no
# longer exists, and the row then failed for the wrong reason (`find:
# No such file or directory`). The new corpus is better than a repointed
# path: `check/` exists to hold refused programs, so this row cannot rot
# for want of a wrong one — and the count is deliberately not pinned
# here, because a new check case must not turn this red.

# The zen is the one verb with no input at all.

test "the tables are what this module is, so they are counted"
    # Counted by running the count, after the first draft asserted two
    # numbers somebody had counted on screen (25 and 8, against 26 and 9).
    # 26 -> 30 on 2026-09-26, defect 102: `lex --dump-tokens --json` over a tab
    # inside a string literal and over a control byte, `check --json` over the
    # same byte, and `check` over characters a terminal cannot show, each
    # pinned where the byte went into JSON raw or into a message unseen.
    # 30 -> 31 on 2026-10-05, defect 291: `lex --dump-tokens` over the same
    # characters a terminal cannot show, each written by its code.
    assert argv_probes().len() == 31

    # 4 -> 5 on 2026-09-28: `probe`'s default and its two flags (panel 179).
    assert help_probes().len() == 5

    # 9 -> 13 on 2026-10-06, defect 271's JSON half (panel 193): a fix's place
    # and first round, withheld and written inside `certain137-inside`,
    # elsewhere in `applyx`, a guess's place with no first round, and a CRLF
    # line's end (defect 373). The commit that added them, `155603c7`, left
    # this count at 9, so the net's own tests read 1 failed until this line.
    assert render_probes().len() == 13

    # 17 -> 19 on 2026-09-04: defect 010's two rows (`ffi-pointee-void`,
    # `ffi-pointee-opaque`). An addition is the direction this assert
    # notices least loudly (CLAUDE.md § Commands), and here it noticed.
    # 19 -> 20 on 2026-09-06, M-declared-freer step 4a: the freer a header
    # refutes. `owned fclose` on a string `strdup` returned used to build
    # clean and die at exit 139; the probe types a marked freer's parameter
    # `char *` and clang refuses it, and the row pins that the caret lands on
    # the MARK and not on the `fclose` declaration, which is correct.
    # 20 -> 21 on 2026-09-19, M-declared-extents step 5: a `ptr` lend from a
    # `=` binding handed to a C function the header lets write. The row pins
    # that the sentence is Heroes' and lands on the LEND, and that neither
    # `internal error` nor clang's `discards qualifiers` reaches the author.
    # 21 -> 22 the same day, step 6: an extent past the field, on a `partial`
    # record among the three shapes. The row pins that the caret lands on the
    # NUMBER and that clang's `static assertion` never reaches the author.
    # 22 -> 23 on 2026-10-02, lane ffi-macro, panel 185 R1: a macro-only name
    # is `ffi_macro_name`, and the row pins that the function its note drafts
    # carries no C type the declaration wrote.
    assert ffi_probes().len() == 23

    # 33 -> 35 on 2026-09-03, M-robustness-guards step 2: `qualifyptr`'s two
    # rows — the program whose `certain` fix reproduced itself, now a guess,
    # and `--apply` leaving its text untouched.
    # 35 -> 36 on 2026-09-03, M-robustness-guards step 4: the deep-recursion
    # row, the first surface row whose exit code is not asserted — `abort()`
    # is 134 on POSIX and the MSVC runtime's own number on Windows, and the
    # message is the fact.
    # 36 -> 40 on 2026-09-03, M-robustness-guards step 6: the four rows of
    # panel 099 R5's nested-leaf fixture.
    # 40 -> 42 on 2026-09-03, M-corpus-depth steps 1 and 2: `mutate` reads a
    # nested program from its root (the corpus was refused for a day because
    # it did not — measurement 014), and the gate over the whole corpus that
    # every leg runs, one cheap operator (typo-digit, 29 mutants, 2.1 s of
    # base check over 78 files measured the day it was written).
    # 42 -> 44 on 2026-09-03, the repair of defect 007: the WIDE-frame
    # recursion at -O0 and -O2. Its -O0 frame is 32 KiB and the prologue
    # probes it with `___chkstk_darwin` before `sp` moves, which is the shape
    # the guard's second witness missed — `heroes check` died at 139 in
    # silence on a legal program. Not under `--sanitize`: the guard yields to
    # ASan there by design, and ASan's own `stack-overflow` report is what
    # `run/`'s judge refuses on sight, so the case cannot live in `run/`.
    # 44 -> 45 on 2026-09-06, M-thread-stacks step 1: the same recursion on a
    # thread the program starts. Before the repair that row's program printed
    # nothing and exited 132 while the row above it named its function at 134 —
    # one program, two threads, two different languages. It asserts the message
    # and not the code for the row above's reason, and because Windows was
    # measured that day to have been right all along: its handler is the
    # process's, where POSIX's alternate stack is the thread's.
    # 45 -> 47 on 2026-09-06, defect 015's repair: a foreign word in CALL
    # position keeps its message and loses its `certain` fix, and `--apply`
    # leaves that program untouched. Two rows because the pair is the claim —
    # one asserts what is said, the other asserts what is NOT done, and §8's
    # machine-applicable promise is only tested by the second.
    # 47 -> 48 the same day, settling `DECIDE.md`'s question about whether the
    # built-in constructors are inside `spec § 9 Functions and calls`'s mandatory-label rule. They
    # are outside it and accept the labels anyway — both spellings compile — so
    # the answer is pinned by a fixture rather than bought with spec tokens.
    # 48 -> 49 on 2026-09-15, defect 037's close: a handle tag that C names with
    # two words, declared in one module and used in another, runs — the
    # qualifier learned from clang's refusal has to reach both translation
    # units, and a golden case cannot hold two modules.
    # 50 -> 52 on 2026-09-25, defect 085's close: a releaser a mark names is
    # read in the mark's own module, checked from the program and from the
    # module alone, and one verdict needs two modules to be seen.
    # 52 -> 53 the same day, M-agreed-retention step 12: two modules that
    # declare one C function differently, which no golden case can hold.
    # 53 -> 56 the same day, defect 095's close: `fmt` over three canonical
    # files whose parameter lists hold a comment, each pinned byte for byte at
    # exit 0. The claim is the self-check's three properties passing on a
    # program the seed refused or silently moved, and a case under `run/` is
    # judged by what it prints, never by `fmt`.
    # 56 -> 59 the same day, defect 096's close: `fmt` over three canonical
    # files, a remark with a blank line under it inside a group, the shapes
    # beside it inside one, and the same shapes outside one, each pinned byte
    # for byte at exit 0. The seed refused the first two at exit 2.
    # 59 -> 64 the same day, landing with it, defects 099 and 100 and the
    # regression 096's first repair made: `fmt` over two same-head groups
    # with a doc above the second, a comment after the last item of every
    # kind of block with and without a blank line under it, a comment the
    # parser did not take as a doc at another column and under a merged
    # head, and a second head kept, each pinned byte for byte at exit 0. The
    # trunk's compiler refused the last four at exit 2.
    # 64 -> 71 the same day, defect 101 with the regression and the `else`
    # the skeptic seat found over that landing: `fmt` over a doc above a
    # second same-head head with a blank line under it and a remark before
    # such a doc, a comment trailing every line that opens a block and a
    # second head, a comment inside a bracket, and a comment between match
    # arms, each pinned byte for byte at exit 0; and a comment `fmt` cannot
    # keep, refused at exit 2 where it was moved at exit 0.
    # 71 -> 78 on 2026-09-26, defect 101's last round: that refusal became a
    # kept comment, and seven files joined it, each pinned byte for byte at
    # exit 0, where the first repair refused or moved every shape in them:
    # a comment on a signature's `(` line, in a block head's value, between
    # a name and its value, inside parentheses, in the part before a
    # bracket, in nested brackets, and on a file's last line twice.
    # 78 -> 85 on 2026-09-26, defect 101's third round, the skeptic seat's
    # findings over 832478b3: a comment after a `)` alone on its line at a
    # block's end, a run with a shallower comment first, a comment trailing
    # `-> ()`, one trailing the author's `)` line, one inside a type, one
    # inside index brackets, and a `)` inside an interpolation's hole, each
    # pinned byte for byte at exit 0 where that commit refused it or moved
    # it out of its block.
    # 85 -> 94 on 2026-09-26, defect 101's fourth round, the second skeptic
    # seat's findings over e0e08d18: a comment inside a written `-> ()`, one
    # after a `.`, one just inside the parentheses around an operand, one
    # after the outer of two parentheses, one in a parameter's or an
    # argument's lead, one inside the result type of a signature with no
    # parameters, a comma between two comments, the pieces of a line broken
    # twice, and a run whose columns climb, each pinned byte for byte at
    # exit 0 where that commit refused it, moved it, or printed it a column
    # too far.
    # 94 -> 100 on 2026-09-26, defect 101's fifth round, the third skeptic
    # seat's findings over 83ac68c1: a comment inside the author's
    # parentheses in an index, two around the outer one, one just inside
    # the outer of two pairs, one around a mutated place, one after a
    # qualified type's dot, and the lines of a block's head, each pinned
    # byte for byte at exit 0 where that commit refused it or printed a
    # continuation at the body's column.
    # 100 -> 101 on 2026-09-27, the same round: panel 179's compiler-engineer
    # ran the seats' bracket-break generator, ported to Heroes, over the
    # fixtures, and a comment just inside the author's parentheses with a
    # unary operator's or an argument's comment inside is pinned here.
    # 101 -> 102 the same day: the continuation-column generator over the
    # new rows found a comma kept on its line after a comment taking a
    # comment further along its line, inside the value after it, and that
    # comma's line is pinned here.
    # 102 -> 121 the same day, M-agreed-retention step 22: fourteen rows for
    # defect 109's empty file, alone, imported and in a corpus, through every
    # verb that reads a program, where `check`, `lex`, `parse`, `build`,
    # `run`, `test` and `mutate` panicked and `fmt` and the IR dump printed a
    # newline for nothing; three for a file that ends inside a declaration,
    # whose parse read on into the next file; and two for comments and calls
    # above ASCII that aborted `fmt` and `check`, found beside defect 110.
    # 121 -> 131 the same day, M-agreed-retention step 23, defects 107 and
    # 108 (counted 102 -> 112 on the lane's own base): five `fmt` rows of a
    # comment the author's parentheses hold, where no other bracket does,
    # in every context that holds an expression, and of a line continued
    # at the statement's margin; and five of a comment inside a releaser
    # set, `check`, `run` and `fmt` over the set and `check` and `run`
    # over two modules declaring one C function, each where the trunk
    # refused it.
    # 131 -> 133 the same day, M-agreed-retention step 24, defect 117
    # (counted 121 -> 123 on the lane's own base): `mutate` on a file and on a path that
    # is not there, each saying what the path is.
    # 133 -> 142 on 2026-09-28, M-agreed-retention step 27, panel 179's
    # probe: eight rows of `heroes probe`'s contract, its report, its exit 1
    # on a file with diagnostics, its seeds under a directory, and its four
    # refusals; and one `fmt` row, the comma with a comment after it that
    # the probe's first run over the fixtures found refused at exit 2.
    # 142 -> 149 the same day, M-agreed-retention step 28, panel 181 (counted
    # 133 -> 140 on the lane's own base): seven rows net, defect 116's margin
    # fixture now refused at exit 1 in place of its row, defect 118's two
    # members at exit 1, defect 119's `(5)` refused once and formatted at exit
    # 0, and a call that begins with `(` after a block running as the second
    # statement it reads as.
    # 149 -> 151 the same day, defect 125: `patterns125/negative.hero`
    # formatted at exit 0 as its own canonical form, where fmt exited 2, and
    # run.
    # 151 -> 156 on 2026-10-01, lane 135c's batch gate, defect 141 (rows added
    # at `fa64c7da`, this count found by the net's own tests at the gate):
    # `check --apply` handing back a clean file whose last line is open, a
    # file of one remark, and a file with a hole, byte for byte, nothing on
    # stdout with `--in-place`, and a module whose last byte is a stray
    # carriage return refused there.
    # 156 -> 162 on 2026-10-02, lane fbrace, defect 173 (panel 184 R1): the
    # walk a new escape owes every tool, over `braces173/main.hero`: `fmt`
    # printing `}}` as written, the token, syntax-tree, scopes and IR dumps
    # each held to one line, and `mutate` scoring the file.
    # 162 -> 166 the same day, panel 184 R2: the note `unused_binding` gives a
    # name a plain literal holds in braces, its `f` a guess, the note alone
    # where the binding does not reach the literal, a rename to `_` made a
    # guess beside it, and no note for `{{taken}}` or a pattern's literal.
    # 166 -> 169 the same day, defect 176 (panel 185 R7): a plain literal whose
    # braces name what is bound refused with its two guesses, each doubling
    # the braces of text, no `unused_binding` beside it, and `--permissive`
    # dropping it as the thesis rule it is.
    # 169 -> 170 on 2026-10-05, defect 355 (panel 192's R7): `heroes test`
    # prints a failing title as its source spells it, so a character a line
    # cannot show reaches its output by its code and never as itself.
    # 170 -> 176 the same day, defect 251 (panel 192's R5): the walk a new
